1. Introduction
Black soldier fly larvae (BSFL; Hermetia illucens (L.); Diptera: Stratiomyidae) can quickly convert organic sidestreams and biowaste into valuable proteins and lipids (Barragan-Fonseca et al., 2017) which can be used in animal nutrition (Bosch and Swanson, 2021; Dörper et al., 2021) and have been increasingly produced and studied by academic and private stakeholders of the insects as feed sector. Both profitability and sustainability of BSFL production depend on their ability to convert feed
provided to the insects into larval biomass. Conversion efficiency varies namely with feed, rearing conditions and genetics (Greenwood et al., 2021). Significant variability of zootechnical performances (development time, prepupal weight, larval protein and lipid contents, etc.) due to the type of feed and its macronutrient composition (mostly carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, fibres and ash) has been extensively documented (Gold et al., 2018; Hopkins et al., 2021). Barragan-Fonseca et al. (2021) used the Nutritional Geometry Framework to comprehensively map the impact of feed protein and carbohydrate content on BSFL
