performance. This work laid the ground for a conceptual diet formulation approach that would allow targeting the appropriate macronutrient profile to reach the expected zootechnical performance of BSFL. However, BSFL fed diets formulated with the same protein and carbohydrate contents but different ingredients showed bioconversion rates of dry mass (DM) ranging from 14.5 to 31.8% (Gold et al., 2020a). One main reason for this variability is the use of crude macronutrient fractions for the formulation instead of the more appropriate digestible macronutrient contents. Indeed the digestibility of a macronutrient can significantly vary from one ingredient to another, as extensively documented in conventional livestock species such as pigs (Stein et al., 2016) or poultry (Zaefarian et al., 2015). Still, data on digestibility by BSFL are lacking.
Apparent digestibility in conventional livestock species is defined on a DM basis for any macronutrient as the amount of macronutrient in ingesta subtracted by macronutrient in faeces, divided by macronutrient in ingesta (Barret et al., 2005). Its calculation requires: (1) estimating the amount of feed ingested; and (2) collecting pure faeces (not contaminated with feed, urine, bedding, etc.) to perform macronutrient content analyses. Ingested feed is usually either determined gravimetrically by comparing the weights of distributed – all the feed given to the animal – and refused feed – non-ingested feed left at the end of the feeding period – or via an undigestible tracer added to the feed and quantified in excreta, such as chromic oxide (McGinnis and Kasting, 1964). However, BSFL feed in aggregation on moist substrates that also constitute their living environment along with various microbes, and excreta cannot be separated from non-ingested feed, exuviae abandoned during moulting and potential dead individuals (Seyedalmoosavi et al., 2022). All these components are mixed together and indistinguishable in a powdery to sticky compost-like material called ‘frass’. therefore, any undigestible tracer would ultimately end up in the frass and collecting reliable data to calculate Apparent Digestibility in BSFL is challenging. A possible partial solution is to attach an excreta collection device to the bottom end of a larva. Still, collected amounts of pure excreta are tiny and this method is impractical on many larvae (Gold et al., 2020b). Furthermore, unlike mammals that produce faeces and urine separately, insects’ metabolic end products are released in the hindgut via Malpighian tubules (Weihrauch and O’Donnell, 2021). Hence their excreta consists of a mix of faeces (undigested feed material) and urine (metabolic end products). Accordingly, Waldbauer (1968) highlighted the difference between Approximate Digestibility, which is calculated based on macronutrient weight in ingesta and in excreta, and Apparent Digestibility, which is similar but requires subtracting the weight of macronutrients in urine from total excreta to obtain the weight of macronutrients in faeces and thus be consistent with the definition of apparent
digestibility in conventional livestock. This refinement is suitable when insects’ excreta can be separated from noningested feed, which is not the case in BSFL. Yet, some authors analysed frass to gain insight into BSFL nutrition but imprecisely referred to approximate digestibility (Klammsteiner et al., 2021; Permana and Ramadhani Eka Putra, 2018). This misuse is due to confusion between distributed and ingested feed and an unclear definition of BSFL excreta and frass (Bosch et al., 2020). In the present paper, we consider excreta as a mixture of faeces and urine, and we use the official definition of frass given by the European Commission: ‘the mixture of insect excrements with parts of dead insects and feeding substrate’ (European Commission, 2021). Hence, we propose a new definition for a digestibility approximation specific to insects growing in their feed: estimated digestibility (ED) is calculated by dividing the difference between distributed feed and frass macronutrient weight by the amount of macronutrient in distributed feed. For insects feeding on moist and microberich substrates like BSFL, ED should be considered as the overall digestion efficiency of insects and the substrate’s microbial community (Schreven et al., 2021).
DM content of frass is often measured in studies focusing on waste management with BSFL to quantify ‘waste reduction’ (sometimes called ‘overall degradation’). These terms have the same definition as ED of DM but are imprecise and unsuitable in the field of animal nutrition. ED of DM ranges from 9 to 85% depending on the type of feed but also rearing conditions, such as feed moisture content, microbes in the substrate, feeding duration or larval density (Lalander et al., 2019; Rehman et al., 2017). The latter’s effect on ED has only been indirectly investigated, with results suggesting that ED is positively associated with larval density (Brits, 2017; Permana and Ramadhani Eka Putra, 2018). This is supported by the hypothesis that a higher number of BSFL is more likely to consume a given amount of feed entirely. It can be assumed that a high density would ultimately result in reaching a maximal ED, corresponding to the full potential of the feed that BSFL and the microbial community can digest. However, this has never been documented. In contrast to ED of DM, values of ED of starch, protein, lipid, fibre and ash are scarce to non-existent.
Consequently, this study proposes a method to assess the maximal ED of a feed for the BSFL conversion process. Null to extremely high larval densities were used, and an extensive analysis of remaining macronutrients in frass was performed to challenge the impact of density on the ED of each macronutrient. This approach aims to consider the maximal ED of a macronutrient as the digestibility coefficient of those specific macronutrient and ingredient when developing a BSFL diet formulation tool.


