2. Materials and methods
Insects and feed used
BSFL were obtained from Agronutris (Toulouse, France). Before the trial, neonate larvae were fed chicken feed (CF) (Maïsadour, Sud-Ouest Aliment VT100 Poulet Jaune Démarrage, Haut-Mauco, France) rehydrated to 64±0.5% moisture. On a DM basis, the CF contained 42.1±1.9% starch (mean and standard-deviation on four samples), 3.4±0.2% nitrogen (four samples), 2.8±0.1% ether extract (EE; two samples), 6.1±0.2% ash (two samples), 14.4±0.9% neutral detergent fibre (NDF; five samples), 5.7±0.7% acid detergent fibre (ADF; five samples), 1.3±0.9% acid detergent lignin (ADL; five samples) and had a gross energy content of 15.8±0.5 kJ/g DM. Pre-experimental BSFL were kept at around 28 °C and 55% relative humidity (RH) for about seven days. After shipping to the laboratory, they were successively sieved with 2.4 and 1.0 mm diameter sieves to get rid of most of the frass and standardise their size by removing over- and under-sized larvae. The larvae were then gently stirred by hand and three samples of about 1 g were taken and counted manually to measure the average fresh mass (FM) of a starter larva (4.9±0.2 mg) and to calculate the overall mass of larvae to use for each experimental density.
Experimental rearing conditions
Experiments were conducted in 16.5×10.6×7 cm rectangular plastic containers (Pro-Jet BG110, Saint-Ouen-l’Aumône, France) individually placed in a knotted light pantyhose and sealed with a lid with an opening of 16×10 cm to prevent larvae from escaping while allowing air circulation. The feed used was CF rehydrated to 68±0.5% moisture using hot tap water and mixed thoroughly (Electrolux Professional Bermixer Pro 450 W, Saint-Denis, France; maximum power for 2:30 min) right before distribution. Each container received a unique input of feed for the whole experimental period, consisting of 420±1 FM g of CF, i.e. 134 DM g, and a specified amount of larvae, covering an extensive range of larval density (Table 1). These experimental conditions were established based on preliminary tests to ensure presumed total feed consumption and sufficient frass DM collection for analyses. Two trials were performed in the same conditions, with two and three replicas per density respectively and slight changes in the investigated larval densities.
Containers were placed in a climatic chamber at 28±0.5 °C and 75±2% RH, with a L12:D12 photoperiod (Trial 1: Xylem Analytics WTW TS 1006-i, Weilheim, Germany; Trial 2: Percival Scientific DR-36VL, Perry, GA, USA). Larvae were kept in their container and allowed to feed for seven days. Containers were relocated eight times to minimise effects related to possible climatic heterogeneity within the chamber. No additional feed input, sampling or mixing was performed during the trial period.
Sampling and analyses
At the end of the seven-day feeding period, all containers were removed from the chamber and weighed. Larvae were manually separated from the frass. Depending on the stickiness of the frass, they were either sieved or removed with forceps. They were rinsed with tap water in a fine sieve, dried with paper towel and weighed collectively. The FM of frass was calculated as the final weight of the container before removing the larvae, subtracted by the weight of the cleaned larvae and the weight of the empty container.
Frass, including material sticking to the inner side of the container, was manually collected and mixed with a spatula. DM content was determined on a 4 g sub-sample using a thermobalance set at 120 °C (Radwag MA 50.R, Radom, Poland). The chemical composition of frass and CF was analysed on oven-dried (Memmert UE500, Schwabach, Germany; 60 °C until constant weight) and ground (Retsch ZM 1000, Haan, Germany; 1 mm grid) samples. Starch content was determined by colourimetry (Roche Cobas Mira S, Basel, Switzerland; Randox GLUC-PAP Kit, Crumlin, UK) after enzymatic hydrolysis (Merck, 101155G-F, Darmstadt, Germany) according to Faisant et al. (1995); nitrogen content was determined using the Dumas method (Elementar EA Vario Micro Cube, Langenselbold, Germany); EE was determined with the Soxhlet method using diethyl ether (Tecator Soxtec System HT6, Höganäs, Sweden); ash content was determined gravimetrically by complete combustion at 550 °C for 8 h (Nabertherm Furnace, Lilienthal, Germany); NDF, ADF and ADL were determined using the filter bag technique (Ankom 220 Fibre Analyzer, Macedon, NY, USA) according to the Van Soest method, after treatment with acetone, α-amylase and pronase to prevent interactions with excess lipids, starch
