For each container, a sample of 30 larvae was weighed on a precision balance (Sartorius AZ153, Göttingen, Germany, ±0.001 g) to calculate the mean individual FM gain and estimate the final number of larvae. The survival rate was calculated as the ratio between final and initial numbers of BSFL. Feed conversion ratio (FCR dry to fresh) was calculated as the ratio between DM of feed distributed and total larval FM gain. Larvae were killed by freezing at -20 °C. Due to improper sample management, no further analysis was performed on Trial 1 larvae. Larvae from Trial 2 were oven-dried at 60 °C until constant weight to calculate their DM content, mean individual DM gain and FCR on a dry basis (FCR dry to dry).
Statistical analysis
Collected data was analysed using R (version 4.1.2., R Core Team, 2021). As there was no difference of treatment between trials, except for a mean difference of recorded temperature of 1.0 °C (27.1 °C for Trial 1, 28.1 °C for Trial 2), all data was pooled together. Considering the pattern of scatter plots of ED of DM, starch, nitrogen, EE, ash and energy, the effect of areal density was modelled for each variable using the nonlinear logistic model ‘logit’ from the ‘drc’ package. The equation of the model is Equation 2, with -1/b the slope for X=0, c the lower asymptote, d the upper asymptote and e the inflexion point:
