3. Results
Estimated digestibility
Figure 1 presents values of ED for each areal density. Increasing areal density between 0 and 11.4 larvae/cm2 positively affected ED of DM (from 3.0±2.0 to 79.7±1.8%), starch (from 24.6±7.8 to 98.7±1.2%), nitrogen (from -12.3±3.4 to 78.4±2.0%), EE (from 9.9±15.3 to 94.8±1.4%), ash (from -0.6±1.6 to 60.8±4.4%) and energy (from 11.0±2.3 to 80.3±1.2%). Higher densities showed similar ED values, resulting in an asymptotic trend, although ED of ash slightly decreased between 11.4 and 28.6 larvae/cm2 from 60.8±4.4% down to 54.0±4.9%. Asymptotic ED determined by the logistic models (parameter d) were 80.3, 99.0, 78.6, 95.3, 58.4 and 80.6% for DM, starch, nitrogen, EE, ash and energy respectively (Table 2).
No effect of areal density on ED of NDF, ADF and ADL could be detected (Figure 1G, H, I). Mean ED of these fractions was 22.6±5.9%, 6.6±6.1% and 11.8±15.6%, respectively.
Life history traits and conversion efficiency
Figure 2 presents the results of life history traits and conversion efficiency. Mean survival rate ranged between 100.2±2.5 and 85.4±3.8% and declined with increasing areal density. Values above 100% are a computational artefact due to a lack of accuracy in estimating the initial and final numbers of BSFL. FCR (dry to fresh) was continuously improved by increasing areal density until reaching a minimum level (0.73±0.04), while FCR (dry to dry) only decreased between 2.3 and 11.4 larvae/cm2 (from 7.30±0.60 to 3.02±0.07) and worsened slightly at higher densities. Both were modelled with an exponential equation: asymptotic value, AD95 and AD99 were 0.78, 8.6 larvae/cm2 and 11.7 larvae/cm2 for FCR (dry to fresh) and 3.15, 5.9 larvae/cm2 and 7.5 larvae/cm2 for FCR (dry to dry) respectively (Table 2). Larval DM content, individual FM gain and individual DM gain reached their maximal value at 4.0 larvae/cm2 (30.3±1.4%, 182.7±13.4 mg and 55.4±6.1 mg, respectively) and decreased with rising areal density.


