4. Discussion
This study proposed a method based on frass analyses and variation of larval density to assess maximal ED in BSFL conversion systems. The results showed that the ED of DM, starch, nitrogen, EE, ash and energy is positively affected by areal density, following a sigmoidal pattern that can be modelled with a logistic curve. High areal densities allowed more BSFL to collectively feed on the same amount of CF, leading to higher ED values, up to an asymptote that indicates the maximal fraction of feed
BSFL and associated microbes can potentially digest. Undigestible macronutrients remaining in the frass were not biologically available for BSFL either because they are physically embedded in a resistant structure (such as proteins in a vegetal cell wall) or because larvae do not have the appropriate enzymes. In CF, BSFL and the associated microbial community were able to digest up to 80.3, 99.0, 78.6, 95.3, 58.4 and 80.6% of DM, starch, nitrogen, EE, ash and energy respectively. ED of DM in CF in the literature ranged between 49.9 and 84.8% (Table 3). Brits (2017) explored the effect of density on ED of DM in kitchen
