waste and, although the experimental set-up did not allow for a clear asymptotic trend, a maximal value of 35.5% was obtained. This result suggests CF to be more digestible than kitchen waste. Values of ED of starch, nitrogen, EE, ash and energy in CF are unavailable in the literature, but some have been reported in other types of feed. ED of nitrogen ranged from 27% (in pig manure) to 72.4% (in soybean curd residue). In a mixture of yeast, wheat and potato byproducts, ED of starch, nitrogen, lipids and energy were 55.8, 37.9, 72.9 and 46.8%, respectively (Table 3). Values of ED from the present study were the highest reported for these macronutrients. However, these results should be compared with caution due to a lack of standardisation in experimental conditions between studies, namely in terms of larval density, feeding time, feeding rate and genetics of the insects used (Bosch et al., 2020; Zhou et al., 2013).
Asymptotic ED in BSFL can also be compared to Apparent Digestibility and Approximate Digestibility in other insect species. Both vary significantly with feed, insect species, rearing conditions and larval instar. In mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor), wheat bran has an Approximate Digestibility of DM of 46% and an Apparent Digestibility of nitrogen, starch and lipids of 54, 59 and 73%, respectively (Waldbauer, 1968). The Apparent Digestibility of wheat in growing pigs and ruminants is 90 and 88% for organic matter, 84 and 70% for nitrogen, 24 and 63% for lipids, and 88 and 86% for energy, respectively (Sauvant et al., 2002). Hence BSFL show strong potential to digest high-quality feed such as CF, comparable to what can be observed in conventional livestock species.
It can be noticed that mean ED of starch slightly decreased between 4.0 and 4.6 larvae/cm2 (from 64.5±6.9 to 42.1±4.6%) and the same phenomenon can also be observed to a lesser extent in ED of other fractions. This is likely due to the way data was pooled, as the 4.0 and 4.6 larvae/cm2 treatments were only present in Trial 2 and Trial 1 respectively. It can be assumed that bioconversion process was slightly improved in Trial 2 where the mean temperature recorded in the climatic chamber was 1.0 °C higher than in Trial 1. However, ED values above 11.4 larvae/cm2 were highly consistent, which suggests that asymptotic ED is a reliable indicator of the digestibility of substrates in BSFL rearing, probably weakly affected by temperature.
In contrast with other macronutrients, ED of NDF, ADF and ADL were not affected by areal density. Mean ED values (22.6±5.9%, 6.6±6.1% and 11.8±15.6%, respectively) were below those of other fractions, suggesting that BSFL are inefficient at digesting fibres. These results are consistent with reported digestive activity in BSFL, which are high for amylase, lipase, and protease but low for cellulase (Intayung et al., 2021; Sun et al., 2022). This also concurs with BSFL developing well on substrates rich in starch and proteins and having low growth performance on fibre-rich substrates
(Gold et al., 2018). However, these very low ED of fibres are inconsistent with results obtained on soybean curd residue, cattle manure and mixtures of both, where ED varied between 49.6 and 69.7% for cellulose, 43.3 and 81.7% for hemicellulose, 24.0 and 39.3% for lignin (Rehman et al., 2017). This difference could indicate that fibre fractions are less digestible in CF than in cattle manure and soybean curd residue. However, low fibre ED from the present study could also result from an inappropriate analysis method. Indeed, it has been reported that the Van Soest method was not specific to vegetal fibre fractions and that ADF could be used to estimate chitin content in shellfish (Stelmock et al., 1985). Although insect and shellfish chitin have structural differences (Brigode et al., 2020), it can be assumed that at least part of the chitin from dead BSFL and exuviae in frass was quantified by the Van Soest method along with plant fibres. It remains unclear whether the Van Soest method is appropriate for estimating residual plant fibre content in frass. Other analysis methods should be explored, such as a pre-treatment of frass samples with chitinase. As chitin contains nitrogen, measuring frass chitin content would also allow a more accurate nitrogen ED determination.
With 0 larvae/cm2, ED was positive for DM (3.0±2.0%), starch (24.6±7.8%), EE (9.9±15.3%) and energy (11.0±2.3%). This is likely due to the activity of microbiota in the substrate that partly digested it to produce microbial biomass. These values should not be considered as a basal microbial activity and used to arithmetically isolate digestion by BSFL and by microbes, as larval activity modifies the microbial community and both affect each other in a process called ‘co-conversion’ (Cifuentes et al., 2020; Schreven et al., 2022), with BSFL consuming and digesting microbial biomass (Gold et al., 2018). On the contrary, 0 larvae/cm2 density resulted in negative ED for nitrogen (-12.3±3.4%) and ash (-0.7±1.6%), which indicated a negative balance between macronutrients removed from and added to the substrate during the trial. Negative ED of nitrogen could result from diazotrophs in the feed that transformed atmospheric dinitrogen into microbial proteins or ammonia released in the substrate (Chan et al., 1994). Without BSFL, the substrate kept a high moisture content: therefore, it can also be hypothesised that it absorbed some of the gaseous and hydrophilic ammonia produced by BSFL and microbes of other containers in the same climatic chamber. Conversely, the pH increase, heat production and larval movement caused by BSFL digestion process resulted in nitrogen loss from the system via ammonia volatilisation (Parodi et al., 2021), hence overestimated ED of nitrogen, presumably more important in high density containers. The increase in ash concentration at null density is very likely due to the mineral content of tap water used for feed preparation. ED of ash reached its maximum at 11.4 larvae/cm2 but decreased with higher densities, indicating that the substrate accumulated minerals. This might be an experimental artefact: as BSFL used to start the trial were


