not completely free from frass, high-density treatments were also inoculated with more frass than those of lower areal density, hence receiving more undigested ash from pre-experimental feeding. Higher frass ash content at high larval density could also result from a higher number of exuviae shed by BSFL, which bioaccumulate minerals in the cuticle in the form of calcium carbonate (Rubio et al., 2022).
Life history traits values obtained were consistent with data from the literature on chicken feed: Liu et al. (2019) reported survival rates between 40 and 93% and Gold et al. (2018) summarised that prepupal FM could range between 99 and 184 mg. Larval DM content were in the range of 17.9 to 38.8% reported by Bosch et al. (2020). In the present study, high areal density caused stronger competition for feed resources, hence lower survival rate and individual FM and DM gain, as already observed (Brits, 2017; Jones et al., 2019; Opare et al., 2022). Dead BSFL could not be visually detected after seven days of feeding and were presumably consumed by other larvae or decomposed into the substrate. Similar adverse effects of density on survival or growth have been reported in other insect species such as Drosophila melanogaster (Henry et al., 2020), Musca domestica (Kökdener and Kiper, 2020) or Tenebrio molitor (Deruytter and Coudron, 2022). Impaired larval DM content observed in the present work at high density suggests that limited access to feed caused BSFL to use their body energy resources by oxidising their fat reserve (Opare et al., 2022). However, FCR (dry to fresh) was improved by increasing density down to an asymptote, indicating that BSFL performance as a group outweighed each individual underperformance. The same occurred with FCR (dry to dry) but only up to 11.4 larvae/cm2. Interestingly, low densities ranging from 0.3 to 2.5 larvae/cm2 are reported not to affect individual FM (Barragan-Fonseca et al., 2018), presumably because the density was too low to induce competition. These contrasting findings suggest that, although increasing density results in competition and possible accumulation of toxic nitrogenous metabolic end products in the substrate like uric acid, urea or ammonia (Henry et al., 2020; Weihrauch and O’Donnell, 2021), it also supports larval growth via mechanisms such as increased substrate temperature and aeration due to BSFL activity, cooperation through extra-oral digestion, or facilitated development of beneficial microbiota (Barragan-Fonseca et al., 2018; Parra Paz et al., 2015).
Differences between AD95 of ED of EE, ash, nitrogen, starch, energy and DM (6.7, 7.0, 7.1, 7.9, 8.1 and 9.0 larvae/ cm2, respectively) indicate that calculated optimal density to fully exploit the nutritional potential of a diet varies with the macronutrient considered. These differences could be related to the amount of each macronutrient in CF: it contained far less EE than starch and it might explain why EE requires fewer larvae to be optimally digested. Starch was the main component and the primary source of energy
in CF. Therefore, differences between AD95 of starch, DM and energy are relatively small. The difference with AD95 of FCR (dry to fresh) (8.5 larvae/cm2) is also low, which highlights that starch is an essential driver of BSFL weight gain (Barragan-Fonseca et al., 2021).
Optimal areal density should be chosen based on the aim of the BSFL farming unit. Insect protein producers should be driven by the FCR (dry to dry), while for waste management stakeholders, insects are a tool for reducing organic waste and ED of DM should be the main driver (Myers et al., 2008). According to present data, densities around 11 larvae/cm2 (Figure 2D and 1A) would produce the best output for these two indicators. On the other hand, individual FM or DM gain is the most important indicator for insect producers looking for heavy BSFL (for reproduction purpose for example), and densities between 2 and 6 larvae/cm2 (Figure 2E and 2F) should be used. These indications are consistent with broad recommendations available in the literature (Surendra et al., 2020). However, optimal densities should also be based on economic considerations as BSFL neonates can be costly to produce. Insect producers must seek a trade-off between maximising the animal performance and minimising the cost of operations.
In the present study, ED had higher variability for densities bellow AD95 than for higher densities, which suggests asymptotic ED determination to be more reliable, repeatable and suitable for comparisons between types of feed than ED measured at lower densities. Therefore, when investigating the impact of a rearing parameter on ED, the maximal ED that can be obtained should be sought and not just one ED value for a given larval density. This asymptotic ED can be determined with a minimum of three different densities, for example 6, 12 and 18 larvae/cm2, which would presumably result in ED below, around and beyond the asymptote respectively. The results of this study were obtained on CF with a 68% moisture content and in specific containers. Further investigations should focus on evaluating asymptotic ED and AD95 for different types of feed, but also for different feeding conditions likely to affect BSFL digestion, such as moisture content (Dzepe et al., 2020), temperature (Harnden and Tomberlin, 2016), shape and size of the container (Miranda et al., 2021), particle size (Brits, 2017) and addition of beneficial microbes (Zhang et al., 2021). The latter represents an excellent opportunity to improve fibre digestion by using cellulolytic bacteria or fungi (Peguero et al., 2022). ED of macronutrients of a standard diet could also be an insightful indicator when developing a genetic selection strategy for black soldier fly (Eriksson and Picard, 2021), as Zhou et al. (2013) showed that strain had an impact on ED of DM.
High-density containers reached a plateau of ED corresponding to the maximal amount of macronutrients


