biologically available for BSFL and the substrate’s microbial community. However, it remains unclear whether the remaining macronutrients were not digested because they were undigestible or because the substrate conditions evolved and inhibited further ingestion by the larvae. Indeed, at the end of the seven-day feeding period, frass in high-density containers was dry and powdery. This texture is inconsistent with BSFL mouthparts, adapted to ingest semi-liquid feed (Bruno et al., 2020). Further investigations on the effect of water supplementation during growth on asymptotic ED are needed. Similarly, as frass was moist and sticky in low-density containers, BSFL might have been able to digest it further with an extended feeding period, leading to an ED closer to its maximal value. This hypothesis is supported by the ED of DM of 67.7% reported on BSFL fed CF with a density of 1.8 larvae/cm2 after nine days (Gold et al., 2020a), far above the value of 29.7% in seven days predicted by the model of the present study.
BSFL bioconversion process is more complex than digestion by monogastric animals or most other insects because BSFL live in their feeding substrate – a mixture of noningested feed, faeces, urine, dead individual and exuviae – which hosts an important microbial community that participates to the digestion (Figure 3). ED calculation is based on chemical analysis of distributed feed and frass and is a suitable indicator for insects living in their feed like BSFL. Yet more accurate digestibility indicators could be developed based on what is done in other insect or livestock species. Ingesta quantification could be fulfilled via the use of an undigestible tracer added to the feed and quantified in pure excreta, such as chromic oxide, allowing for Approximate Digestibility calculation (Equation 4)
(McGinnis and Kasting, 1964). Collecting pure excreta could either be done with an excreta collection device (Gold et al., 2020b), by separating the top and bottom ends of BSFL by a perforated wall to allow them to feed from a compartment and release excreta in another one, or by putting clean BSFL in an empty container after a feeding period. However, all options lead to tiny excreta samples, are impractical on many insects, possibly stressful for the larvae with an impact on digestion, and could impair typical BSFL bioconversion processes (i.e. larval aggregation, microbial development, heat generation). Dissection of BSFL digestive tract to collect faeces should be avoided as the collected material may not be fully digested (Guillaume et al., 1999). Approximate Digestibility has also been assessed in some insects via uric acid quantification. Dividing uric acid concentration in frass by uric acid concentration in pure excreta allows to determine the amount of excreta in frass, given that the feed does not contain uric acid (Bhattacharya and Waldbauer, 1969). Nevertheless, this method faces several limitations when applied to BSFL as its development also requires pure excreta collection, because some substrates distributed to BSFL such as poultry manure contain uric acid (Amanullah et al., 2007) and finally because in moist and microbe-rich environment such as BSFL frass, uric acid would be quickly transformed in other nitrogenous compounds such as urea and ammonia (Henry et al., 2020).
