In insects producing excreta that can be separated from other frass constituents, uric acid can also be quantified in excreta to calculate Apparent Digestibility (Equation 5), similarly to what is done in birds (Waldbauer, 1968). Yet the molecular form of nitrogenous metabolic end products excreted by Diptera remains unclear (Henry et al., 2020) and further work is needed on urinary materials in frass and their degradation products, i.e. uric acid, allantoin, urea, ammonia, nitrite and nitrate (Barragan-Fonseca et al., 2021). Materials produced in the digestive tract, also called ‘endogenous faeces’ (i.e. microbial biomass, enzymes and gut cells) (Figure 3), could also be quantified to calculate True Digestibility (Equation 6) (Barret et al., 2005). However, this indicator was developed on animals producing an important amount of pure easy-to-collect faeces, such as poultry or pigs, and is complex to assess in small insects living in groups inside their feed. Estimated, approximate, apparent and true digestibilities (Equations 1, 4, 5 and 6, respectively) have close yet different definitions for BSFL and reflect both a rising precision in digestibility assessment and increasing technical issues. In vitro simulation of BSFL digestion could be promising but is limited by the understanding of the enzymatic and microbial activity of the digestive tract (Gold et al., 2020c).
5. Conclusions
The present work proposes a method to evaluate BSFL feed digestion efficiency via asymptotic ED, a new indicator defined as the maximal fraction of initial macronutrients that are not in the frass at the end of the feeding period. ED of major macronutrients are assessed on a standard diet with a focus on the effect of larval areal density. Asymptotic ED corresponds to the maximal proportion of each macronutrient that BSFL and associated microbial community can potentially digest. Therefore, when formulating a diet for BSFL based on several ingredients, crude macronutrient content multiplied by the asymptotic ED of that macronutrient should be considered for each ingredient, rather than the sole crude macronutrient content. Further studies should focus on determining asymptotic ED of macronutrients in different types of ingredients that could be used as BSFL feed (such as agricultural side-streams, food waste or animal manure) and evaluate the effect of rearing conditions on asymptotic ED.
